That brings us to the important issue: utilization.

4. Why the same token can cost vastly different amounts

Think back to your roads-and-traffic observation.

A highway doesn’t cost the same amount to operate per vehicle whether it carries 10 cars an hour or 10,000.

Similarly, a GPU’s infrastructure costs are distributed across all the useful computation it performs.

One user could have an expensive processor mostly to themselves. Or the server could batch dozens of requests together, increasing total token throughput.

The number of tokens produced per second across all users is a crucial variable.

Other important variables include model size, active parameters, numerical precision, context length, and required response speed.

For a simplified dense neural network with 70 billion parameters, a forward pass involves roughly 140 billion floating-point operations per generated token just for the main parameter computations, before additional attention-related work. Optimizations, batching, and model architectures can substantially change actual efficiency.

Tokens are therefore not equivalent units of physical effort. They’re closer to a standardized meter placed over a complicated resource-consumption process.

5. What OpenAI actually charges versus physical cost

For comparison, OpenAI’s published standard API prices for GPT-6 Sol include:

Token category	Published price per 1 million
Input tokens	$2.00
Cached input tokens	$0.20
Output tokens	$10.00

These are customer prices, not disclosed marginal costs. They don’t reveal OpenAI’s actual energy usage, infrastructure cost, or profit per token. Your ChatGPT subscription also does not work like a simple per-message retail API charge.

Notice how output tokens are more expensive than input tokens. That reflects, in part, the difference between parallel prompt processing and sequential generation. Cached inputs can be cheaper because previously computed information can be reused.

Reasoning models may also process internal tokens that aren’t displayed in the finished response. Those can consume computation too.

6. The really interesting implication

You could run a small open-weight AI model locally on a computer you already own.

You wouldn’t pay a company per token.

You would still consume electricity and incur wear and opportunity costs on hardware you’ve bought, but no one would be charging you for each unit of generated text.

So the monetary price isn’t intrinsic to the token.

There are several layers:

\text{Tokens}\rightarrow\text{Operations}\rightarrow\text{Energy and time}\text{Energy + hardware + operations}\rightarrow\text{Economic cost}\text{Economic cost + business decisions}\rightarrow\text{Price}

And those relationships can change radically with technology.

A more efficient algorithm could perform the same task using fewer tokens or less computation per token.

A better chip could perform the same calculations using less energy.

A smaller specialized model could perform some tasks nearly as well while consuming a fraction of the resources.

Or an entirely different architecture might make sequential token-by-token generation less central.

Here’s the invariant I’d extract for Pantheon:

The price of a unit of information is not the same as the physical cost of representing, processing, or producing that information.

That distinction becomes extremely important when we start thinking about compressing models, transferring knowledge, designing AI-native languages, and deciding which computations are actually necessary.

And perhaps the most interesting question beyond cost is this:

How much of the computation required to generate intelligence is genuinely necessary, and how much is simply a consequence of the architecture we’ve chosen to build?

That’s where efficiency research could produce changes far more significant than just making electricity cheaper.